        /// Updates the settings of an existing session. Changes apply to this session only; the agentconfig the session was created from is not modified.Each request makes one of two kinds of update, and the two can&apos;t be combined:- **Session preferences**: set `resume_on_topoff`. Applied immediately; returns `200`.- **Session settings**: set `manifest_yaml` to a partial manifest that holds only the sections  to change. Applied in the background; returns `202`.An empty body (`{}`) returns `400`.### Updatable session settings| Section | What it changes | Agent restart || --- | --- | --- || `tools` | The session&apos;s Action Gateway tools (`do.actions` entries). Custom MCP servers can&apos;t be changed. | No || `env` | Environment variables (name and value). | Yes || `env.HARNESS_INFERENCE_MODEL` | The model, for sessions that use DigitalOcean Serverless Inference. | Yes || `env.MODEL`, `env.OPENAI_MODEL`, `env.ANTHROPIC_MODEL` | The model, for sessions that use their own OpenAI-compatible or Anthropic endpoint. | Yes || `secrets` | Plain secrets (name and value). | Yes || `secrets` (URL-bound) | URL-bound secrets: value, `url`, and `scheme`. | No || `secrets` (OAuth) | The provider connection an OAuth secret uses (`actor`). | Yes || `egress` | The hosts and IP addresses the session can reach (`allow_hosts` and `allow_ips`). | No |Sections you omit are not changed. Each section you include replaces that section in full; see`manifest_yaml` for the exact rules.### Changing the modelYou change a session&apos;s model through `env`; a top-level `model` section returns `400`. The keyto set depends on the model endpoint the session was created with:| Session created with | Model endpoint | `env` key that sets the model || --- | --- | --- || `HARNESS_INFERENCE_MODEL`, and none of the keys below | DigitalOcean Serverless Inference | `HARNESS_INFERENCE_MODEL` || `OPENAI_BASE_URL` | Your OpenAI-compatible endpoint | `MODEL`, or `OPENAI_MODEL` || `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`, or an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret | Your Anthropic endpoint, or the Anthropic API | `ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, or `MODEL` |A session keeps the model endpoint it was created with. Its provider and base URL can&apos;t bechanged (`422`). The provider API key (`OPENAI_API_KEY` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) can be rotatedbut not added, removed, or moved to another URL or scheme (`422`). See `manifest_yaml` for therules.The following can&apos;t be changed on an existing session: custom MCP servers (`422`), the egressVPC (`vpc_uuid` and `subnet_uuid`, `422`), platform-managed model keys, and `permissions`(`400`). To use a different custom MCP server or VPC, create a new session.### Switching an OAuth connectionAn OAuth secret that names a provider connection with `actor` can move to another connection ofthe same provider while the session runs. `actor` is the connection&apos;s `user_id`; see[`GET /v2/harness-runtime/auth/{provider}/connections`](#operation/harness_list_provider_connections).Send the secret with `source`, `provider`, and the new `actor`, and keep every other secret as`{}`. See `manifest_yaml` for an example.The platform first checks that you can use the new connection: it must be active, and eitheryours or shared with your team (`400` otherwise). Switching back to the connection the agentconfig names skips this check, so any team member can do it. The platform then gives the sessionthat connection&apos;s token and restarts the agent, so the next `git` operation uses the newconnection. Sending the connection the secret already uses returns `200` with `outcome` set to`UNCHANGED`.The switch applies to this session only:- New sessions from the same agent config still start on the connection the config names.- A rollback, or a fork from a checkpoint, uses the connection the session had when the  checkpoint was taken.- The switch changes only the token in the session. Action Gateway tools (`do.actions` entries)  keep using the connection Action Gateway picks for the session, so `git` and those tools can  act as different accounts.- A token the agent already read stays valid with the provider until the old connection is  revoked.Switching OAuth connections must be enabled for your team (`403` otherwise). The followingstill require a new session (`422`): adding or removing an OAuth secret, changing its provider,and switching an OAuth secret that was created without `actor`.### URL-bound secrets and egressURL-bound secret and `egress` changes take effect on the session&apos;s next outbound connection.An update that changes only URL-bound secrets and `egress` doesn&apos;t restart the agent and ends in`APPLIED`. Connections that are already open stay open, including connections to a host thatthe update removed.These updates have the following additional requirements:- URL-bound secret and egress updates must be enabled for your team. Otherwise, the request  returns `403`.- The session must have been created with a URL-bound secret or an egress allowlist. A session  created with unrestricted egress and no URL-bound secrets can&apos;t gain either one: the update  ends in `FAILED` with `error` set to `requires_new_session`.- Changing `egress`, or adding a URL-bound secret for a host the session can&apos;t reach yet,  requires that the session&apos;s agent runtime supports egress updates. Sessions created before  egress updates were available don&apos;t support them (`409`).See `manifest_yaml` for the format of each change.### Tracking an update1. A request that changes the session settings returns `202` with `outcome` set to `ACCEPTED`.   The session status becomes `SESSION_STATUS_UPDATING`.2. Poll [`GET /v2/harness-runtime/sessions/{session_id}`](#operation/harness_get_session)   every 2 to 5 seconds until the status is `SESSION_STATUS_READY`, or `SESSION_STATUS_PAUSED`   if the session was paused when you sent the update. The session returns to that status   whether the update succeeded or failed.3. Read `session.update.state`:   - `APPLIED`: the update is live.   - `APPLIED_RESTARTED`: the update is live and the agent was restarted to apply it.   - `FAILED`: the update did not take effect. `session.update.detail` explains why, and     `session.update.reverted` is `true` if the previous settings were restored.Tool, URL-bound secret, and `egress` updates usually finish within a few seconds. Updates to`env` (including the model) or plain secrets, and OAuth connection switches, restart the agentand can take up to about two minutes.If the request matches the session&apos;s current settings, it returns `200` with `outcome` set to`UNCHANGED` and nothing is applied.### VersioningEach accepted update is recorded with its own `update_id` and the manifest hashes before andafter the change (`session.update.previous_hash` and `session.update.manifest_hash`). When anupdate fails, the session is rolled back to its previous settings and `session.update.reverted`is `true`. Once a session&apos;s settings differ from its agent config, the session reports`config_drifted: true`, `config_diff` lists the changed entries, and `manifest` holds thesettings the session now runs with (without secret values).### Requirements- The session status must be `SESSION_STATUS_READY`, `SESSION_STATUS_DETACHED`, or  `SESSION_STATUS_PAUSED`. A paused session is resumed to apply the update and paused again  afterwards, unless it was paused for zero balance. Any other status returns `409`, including  `SESSION_STATUS_PROVISIONING`, `SESSION_STATUS_UPDATING`, a session paused for zero balance,  and a destroyed or failed session. A locked session returns `423`.- The session must be idle: no agent turn running and no approval pending (`409`).- Session settings updates must be enabled for your team.- Not every agent runtime supports every section. An unsupported change returns `409`.
